fc8a0ac3 6qzoi6V6dcFi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 102:82a7616e63686f727391da00492f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f70726f7669646572732f636c617564655f636f64652e676f3a3136373a312d3339373a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d107c2 This is the Query method of ClaudeCodeProvider, which annotates a rooted observation graph with LLM-generated explanations. It first walks the graph via BFS from the root, collecting every vertex (aborting early if the context is cancelled or a vertex lookup fails, surfacing that via innerErr), then fetches all edge keys and resolves each into a full edge. Under the workspace mutex it builds a batch request: for each vertex it computes an ObservationKey from the vertex hash and query; on a cache hit it writes the cached answer directly onto the graph as the 'observation-answer' vertex attribute and skips the LLM; otherwise it assigns a short sequential id like 's0', records the id-to-hash mapping in verteximap, and appends the vertex source text to the batch input. Edges are handled analogously with 'c0'-style ids, a derived edge hash key ('edge:<src>:<dst>'), a cache check that sets the edge attribute, and a human-readable '<src> loads <dst>' description. If every item was served from cache it returns nil without calling the LLM. Otherwise it constructs a strict system prompt (no code changes, factual observations, raw JSON, one output entry per input entry, self-contained answers), marshals the batch input and an empty BatchObservationSchema sample to communicate the output shape, and invokes QueryClaudeCode with 'high' effort expecting a *common.BatchObservationSchema back. On response, again under the mutex, it maps each returned observation id back through verteximap/edgeimap; unknown ids record a ProviderOutputError (only the first error is kept) and are skipped. For valid entries it rewrites the id to the stable cache-key string, resolves the vertex (or both edge endpoints) to build FileRange anchors, 