fc8a0ac3 463sv4NO82Bi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 89:82a7616e63686f727391da003c2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f7374617475732e676f3a313a312d3133393a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10560 Go package `tui` implementing a context-scoped status reporting system for a terminal overlay. A `StatusProvider` wraps an `*Overlay` and guards a shared `*Timer` plus a `tasks` string stack with a `sync.Mutex`. `WithStatusProvider` stores a new provider in a context under a private typed key (`statusContextKeyType`) to avoid key collisions; `GetStatusProvider` retrieves it via a type assertion. The package-level helpers `ReportStatus` and `PushStatusTask` are no-op safe: if no provider is in the context they return immediately (or return an empty cleanup func). `PushTask` lazily creates and starts the timer on the first task, appends the status to the stack, shows the overlay, and renders the status; it returns a cleanup closure protected by `sync.Once` so calling it multiple times only pops once. `popTask` removes the top task; if tasks remain it re-renders the new top status (restoring the previous status, LIFO semantics), otherwise it stops and clears the timer and hides the overlay. Note the mutex is released before calling `p.report`/`p.overlay` methods, keeping I/O outside the critical section. `report` builds a shimmer-decorated status line ('⚓ captn' + status, plus the timer section when non-nil) and pushes it to the overlay via `SetStatus`. Methods also tolerate nil receivers (`if p == nil`), making the whole API safe to call unconditionally.
fc8a0ac3 1Hpf0JEp5Z1i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 91:82a7616e63686f727391da003e2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f7374617475732e676f3a3131343a312d3132343a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10258 `ReportStatus` method on `*StatusProvider`: updates the displayed status text without modifying the task stack. It first guards against a nil receiver (no-op if `p == nil`), then briefly acquires the mutex only to snapshot the current `*Timer` pointer, releases the lock, and calls the unexported `report(status, timer)` which renders the status (with the timer section if one is running) to the overlay. Taking the timer snapshot under the lock ensures a consistent read while keeping the rendering call outside the critical section; the `StatusType` parameter is accepted but not used in rendering.
