fc8a0ac3 z_kK0CS-kMxi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6d63702f6578706c61696e5f746f6f6c2e676f3a313a312d39343a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10483 This file defines an MCP 'explain' tool in package mcp. ExplainInput carries a workdir-relative filePath and a code snippet; ExplainOutput carries the explanation text and operation duration in milliseconds, constructed via NewExplainOutput. ExplainTool implements a Tool interface (verified by the `var explainTool Tool[ExplainInput, ExplainOutput]` assertion) exposing Name() 'explain', DisplayName(), and Description(). Its Call method does the real work: it records a start time, resolves the current working directory, opens a COG workspace over it with cog.OpenWorkspace, constructs a Claude Code LLM provider, locates the snippet in the file via g.SearchSnippet (yielding an observation graph and a start node), then runs og.QueryWithDepth with queries.NewExplainBehaviorQuery() at depth 1 to produce the explanation. Every failure path returns a zero-valued output plus a wrapped error. After success it fires a goroutine to g.Persist() the graph asynchronously (errors merely printed, noted as a TODO because prints clash with claude stdout), computes elapsed milliseconds, and returns the explanation and duration with a nil *mcp.CallToolResult.
fc8a0ac3 pCYP6LNo7QRi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 95:82a7616e63686f727391da00422f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6d63702f6578706c61696e5f746f6f6c2e676f3a34373a312d39313a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10431 Call is the ExplainTool's MCP handler. Flow: (1) build a zero-value ExplainOutput used on all error returns; (2) capture time.Now() for duration measurement; (3) os.Getwd() to find the working directory, wrapping failure as 'invalid CWD'; (4) cog.OpenWorkspace(cwd) to open the code observation graph workspace; (5) create a Claude Code provider via providers.NewClaudeCodeProvider(); (6) g.SearchSnippet(ctx, input.FilePath, input.Snippet) to build an ObservationGraph and identify the start node for the requested snippet, wrapping errors as 'failed to query snippet'; (7) og.QueryWithDepth(ctx, g, prov, start, queries.NewExplainBehaviorQuery(), 1) to have the LLM explain behavior traversing one level of depth. On success it launches a background goroutine calling g.Persist(), only printing persistence failures (a TODO notes stdout collision with claude output — the write is fire-and-forget, so Call may return before persistence finishes). Finally it computes elapsed milliseconds and returns NewExplainOutput(expln, dur) with a nil CallToolResult and nil error.
fc8a0ac3 3y-CgfosHwhi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f636f672f776f726b73706163652e676f3a3533313a312d3637323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10610 SearchSnippet builds a one-level ObservationGraph around a snippet of code. Steps: load the target file through the workspace (cached loader), locate the snippet's byte range with FindSnippetRange, and collect AST nodes inside that range plus the file's Module as root. It then starts an LSP client for the file's language and, for every ASTCallExpression among the in-range children, records the call symbol's position. Those positions are sent as a single batched textDocument/definition request (lsp.NewDefinitionBatchRequest with the full buffer as a TextDocumentItem). Each returned location (possibly several per range, kept for future compatibility) is converted into a ResolvedDependency linking an internal range to an external file range, with descriptive errors on failure. The external files are preloaded in bulk via LoadFiles so subsequent per-dependency LoadFile calls hit cache. For each dependency it asserts exactly one AST node exists at both the internal and external ranges (erroring otherwise), appends the external node to the child set, and records a linkage from the external node to the nearest node-of-interest ancestor of the internal node (ichains). All collected children are collapsed to their nearest node-of-interest into a set. Finally it constructs a cgraph graph keyed by NodeHasher, adds root as a vertex, adds each interest node, and wires edges: from the linked upstream node when the node appears in ichains (cross-file dependency edge), otherwise from root. Returns the ObservationGraph, the root node, and nil.
fc8a0ac3 CxUCtVsj9U1i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 163: This is a file-load edge: the snippet-search routine, running inside the explain tool's workspace, loads another source file. During SearchSnippet, LSP definition results for call expressions in the snippet resolve to external files, and those files are fetched through wspace.LoadFiles/LoadFile (cache-backed) so their ASTs can be parsed and the referenced definition nodes located. The loaded file's matching node becomes a depth-1 vertex in the observation graph, linked by an edge to the calling node of interest in the original file.
fc8a0ac3 fGw2_syYPLFi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 162: This edge records that the same snippet-resolution pass loads an additional dependency file. When SearchSnippet batches textDocument/definition lookups for the snippet's call expressions, each resolved location may live in a different file; every such file is preloaded via wspace.LoadFiles and then re-opened from cache with LoadFile to extract exactly one AST node at the resolved range. That external node is appended to the graph and connected via ichains to the nearest node-of-interest in the source file, giving the explain query cross-file context at depth 1.
fc8a0ac3 CVsf6cUDr99i5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 93:82a7616e63686f727391da00402f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f706172736572732f77616c6b65722e676f3a36383a312d38353a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10322 GetNthChildByKind scans the named children of a tree-sitter ParserNode looking for the (n+1)-th child whose Kind() equals the given kind string, treating n as a zero-based index. It iterates i from 0 to NamedChildCount(), skips nil children, and increments a matches counter for each child of the matching kind. Because matches becomes 1 when the 0th match is found, the condition matches > n fires exactly when the nth (zero-indexed) match is reached; at that point it wraps the raw child in a new ParserNode via NewParserNode(apn.Source, child) — preserving the same source reference — and returns it with true. If the loop completes without n+1 matches (kind absent or fewer occurrences), it returns the zero-value ParserNode and false, so callers must check the boolean before using the result.
fc8a0ac3 0bArjsEfjohi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 135:82a7616e63686f727391da006a2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f2e636c617564652f776f726b74726565732f6167656e742d61396236326337623731626135633064622f706b672f706172736572732f77616c6b65722e676f3a36383a312d38353a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d102a9 GetNthChildByKind searches a ParserNode's named children (via the underlying tree-sitter node's NamedChildCount/NamedChild iteration) for the (n+1)th child whose Kind() matches the given kind string. It counts matches as it iterates; because n is a zero-based index, the check `matches > n` fires exactly when the counter reaches n+1 (e.g. matches=1 satisfies n=0). On a hit it wraps the raw child with NewParserNode(apn.Source, child), preserving the parent's Source, and returns (node, true). Nil children are skipped defensively. If fewer than n+1 matching children exist, it returns a zero-valued ParserNode and false, so callers must check the boolean before using the result.
fc8a0ac3 6fkohibIiq_kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 100:82a7616e63686f727391da00472f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f74657374732f7365617263685f736e69707065745f746573742e676f3a31323a312d33333a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d104e0 This Go test verifies that Workspace.SearchSnippet roots its result at the enclosing function rather than at the file/module level. It obtains the current working directory, constructs a cog.Workspace rooted there, and calls SearchSnippet against fixtures/golang/multidep/cmd/main.go with the snippet text fmt.Println(fixture_dep1.GetExampleText()). SearchSnippet returns an observation graph (og) plus a root node; the test bails out early via an assert.NoError guard so later assertions never run against nil results if the search fails. It then type-asserts the root to *ast.ASTFuncExpression, confirming the returned root is the function that encloses the matched statement (the failure message prints the actual type via %T). Finally it loads the same file with LoadFile, retrieves the adjacency map of og.Graph, and asserts the map does NOT contain f.Module.GetHash() — a negative check proving the module-level node is excluded from the graph. Together the two assertions establish the contract: snippet search scopes its graph to the enclosing function, verified both by the root's type and by the absence of the module hash among the graph's vertices. The test uses testify assert throughout and t.Context() for cancellation-aware calls.
fc8a0ac3 O7wS2-N1Iu7kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f717565726965732f71756572792e676f3a313a312d3136393a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d104e1 Defines a `queries` package modeling LLM prompt variants behind a `PromptQuery` interface (GetID, GetHash, GetPrompt, GetRoutingDescription, GetDisplayHints(provider)). Three stateless empty-struct implementations exist — ExplainBehaviorQuery, ExplainContractQuery, ListSideEffectsQuery — each returning a fixed ID, a static prompt string, a routing description used for query selection, and a six-element hint list whose first and last entries interpolate the provider name via fmt.Sprintf. Identity/caching: defaultPromptIdentifierProvider hashes (id, prompt) with common.HashMany so two queries sharing prompt text still get distinct hashes; ObservationKey combines the node hash string and the query hash in separate positional slots, relying on HashMany being position-dependent so distinct (node, prompt) pairs cannot collide by commutation. Registry helpers: Supported() builds a fresh slice of all three query values per call (no shared mutable state), SupportedIDs() maps it to IDs, and ByID() linearly scans returning (query, true) on match or (nil, false) otherwise. The trailing `var _ PromptQuery = ...` declarations are compile-time conformance assertions, marked banstructlit:ignore to exempt them from a struct-literal lint rule.
fc8a0ac3 AaQ9BqxMLU3kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f717565726965732f71756572792e676f3a32303a312d32323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d101f6 Defines the fallback strategy for deriving a prompt identifier: it passes both the supplied id and prompt string into common.HashMany, which folds the two values into a single hash, then renders it via .String(). Behavior is deterministic — the same (id, prompt) pair always yields the same identifier, while a change in either input yields a different one. Being unexported and named 'default...Provider', it acts as the built-in implementation used when no custom identifier provider is configured.
fc8a0ac3 4z-8dgdg7j3kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f717565726965732f71756572792e676f3a36373a312d36393a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d1012d Value-receiver method on ExplainBehaviorQuery that computes an identity/cache key by delegating to defaultPromptIdentifierProvider with the query's ID and prompt text. The hash therefore changes if either the identifier or the prompt wording changes, giving prompt-version-aware caching/deduplication.
fc8a0ac3 b1RDfB-GSbTkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f717565726965732f71756572792e676f3a36333a312d36353a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d100fc Value-receiver accessor returning the constant identifier string "explain_behavior". Serves as a stable registry/lookup key distinguishing this query from other prompt query implementations; reads no state, so it is effectively a compile-time constant.
fc8a0ac3 oIdsB03JUlHkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f717565726965732f71756572792e676f3a37353a312d37373a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d100f6 Value-receiver accessor returning the hardcoded instruction text asking for an explanation of a code snippet's behavior in concise reasoning format. Because it is a literal, the prompt is deterministic and feeds into the query's hash computation.
fc8a0ac3 NssO-w8qpcPkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 158: Load edge from the ExplainBehaviorQuery container node to one of its bound methods; the loaded member is part of the ID/prompt/hash accessor set that lets the type satisfy the prompt-query interface.
fc8a0ac3 8C_kT5GvwIHkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 158: Load edge from the ExplainBehaviorQuery definition to another of its methods. Collectively these loaded methods supply the identifier, the prompt string, and the hash derived from both, as required by the query abstraction.
fc8a0ac3 xO7bOSb36sHkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 158: Third load edge from the query type to a remaining method implementation, completing the trio of value-receiver accessors: constant ID, literal prompt text, and hash derived from those two values.
fc8a0ac3 FTeLp2NrQ8HkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 90:82a7616e63686f727391da003d2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f636f672f717565726965732e676f3a32303a312d34373a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d1044b Lazily starts and memoizes one LSP client per (languageID, workspace) pair. Steps: build cache key from lang.GetLanguageID() and workspace; take a package-level mutex for the whole function (deferred unlock) so concurrent callers serialize — making the check/install/start/store sequence atomic and preventing duplicate server processes, at the cost of blocking other callers during a slow install or start. On cache hit it returns the existing client immediately. On miss, EnsureLSPServerInstalled runs with the caller's ctx so installation remains cancelable by the originating request, then lsp.Start is deliberately given context.Background() because the cached client outlives that request and must not die when the request ctx is canceled. Start options carry the workspace, client name 'captn-lsp-client', version '0.1.0', and the language's NewLSPServer factory. Start errors are wrapped with the language ID via %w to preserve unwrapping; on success the client is stored in the cache map and returned. Only successes are cached, and there is no eviction or shutdown path in this function.
fc8a0ac3 0QNlE1iyM3TAW6nbcc8nEQAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 111:82a7616e63686f727391da00522f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6c616e6775616765732f676f6c616e672f676f6c616e675f7061727365722e676f3a3235393a312d3236363a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10385 Pure accessor on *GolangLanguageSupportDefinition returning a value-typed lsp.ServerRequirement describing the gopls server: Name from goplsName, InstallCommand from goplsInstallCommand, Locate set to the goplsPath function value. Contract: no parameters, no I/O, no validation, no error return — any failure to locate or install gopls is deferred to when the caller invokes Locate/InstallCommand. Output is deterministic, built fresh from package-level constants/functions on every call; no caching, no receiver mutation. The receiver is never dereferenced, so calling on a nil pointer would not panic; there are no empty/zero input cases since there are no arguments. Zero-value risk is upstream: empty goplsName/goplsInstallCommand or a nil goplsPath are propagated unchecked into the struct. The `banstructlit:ignore` comment only suppresses a struct-literal lint rule and has no runtime effect.
fc8a0ac3 CwtV5yproITAW6nbcc8nEQAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 93:82a7616e63686f727391da00402f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6c73702f726571756972656d656e742e676f3a313a312d32333a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d1052a Declaration-only snippet in package `lsp`; no executable logic. Exports two things. (1) `ErrServerMissing`, a sentinel error built with `errors.New("missing language server")`, meant for `errors.Is` comparison: it marks a language-wide failure (no server binary available for that language) so a caller fanning out across many file matches reports the error rather than silently returning zero resolutions. (2) `ServerRequirement`, a plain struct with `Name` and `InstallCommand` strings — descriptive metadata the caller hands back to the driving agent, since this package never installs anything itself — plus `Locate func(ctx context.Context) (string, error)`, the single resolver for "which binary", intentionally reused by both the missing-server report path and the server-start path so the two cannot disagree. `Locate`'s contract: accepts a context for cancellation/deadline, returns the binary path with nil error on success, or an error when the binary cannot be found; mapping that error to `ErrServerMissing` is left to the caller — nothing here wraps or swallows it. Zero/nil behavior is unguarded: a zero-value `ServerRequirement` carries empty strings and a nil `Locate`, and invoking a nil `Locate` panics, so construction sites must populate it. No constructor, validation, or defaults are supplied.
fc8a0ac3 IwRTTF8rwP7kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 97:82a7616e63686f727391da00442f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6c616e6775616765732f6c616e6775616765732e676f3a313a312d32333a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10430 Defines a `languages` package declaring a `LanguageSupport` interface that abstracts per-language tooling: `Parse` (turns a tree-sitter parse tree plus source into an `*ast.ASTModule`), `NewLSPServer` and `GetLSPServerRequirement` (spawning and describing the language server process), `GetLanguageID` (identifier string), `ClassifyImportType` (maps a source to a `common.DependencyType`, e.g. stdlib vs external vs local), and `GetTreeSitterLanguage` (grammar handle for parsing). It then binds a package-level variable `Golang`, declared with the interface type, to the concrete implementation returned by `languages_golang.NewGolangLanguageSupportDefinition()`. Behavior: the assignment runs at package initialization, so importing this package eagerly constructs the Go language definition; declaring the var as the interface type acts as a compile-time assertion that the Go implementation satisfies all six methods, and lets callers depend on the abstraction instead of the golang subpackage. Additional languages would follow the same pattern via new exported vars.
fc8a0ac3 0QNlE1iyM3TkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 111:82a7616e63686f727391da00522f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6c616e6775616765732f676f6c616e672f676f6c616e675f7061727365722e676f3a3235393a312d3236363a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d102b4 Method on GolangLanguageSupportDefinition implementing a language-support hook: it returns a value-typed lsp.ServerRequirement describing how to obtain the Go LSP server (gopls). It wires three package-level identifiers — goplsName (server identifier), goplsInstallCommand (command used to install gopls when missing), and goplsPath (a Locate function/value resolving the binary on disk). No receiver state is read and no error is returned, so it is a pure, side-effect-free descriptor lookup; the caller performs the actual locate/install/launch. The `// banstructlit:ignore` comment suppresses a lint rule that flags composite struct literals, marking this partial literal as intentional.
fc8a0ac3 CwtV5yproITkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 93:82a7616e63686f727391da00402f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6c73702f726571756972656d656e742e676f3a313a312d32333a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d10365 Declares package `lsp` with two exported items. `ErrServerMissing` is a sentinel error created with `errors.New`, representing the language-server-absent failure; per the comment it is language-wide rather than file-specific, so a caller fanning out over many matched files reports it instead of quietly resolving nothing. `ServerRequirement` is a plain value struct describing what a language needs: `Name` (server identifier), `InstallCommand` (a command string handed back to the driving agent, since this package never installs anything itself — the agent owns the terminal and its own approval flow), and `Locate`, a context-aware function returning the binary path or an error. Because `Locate` is the single lookup used both to detect a missing server and to start it, the two paths cannot disagree. No runtime logic here — purely type/contract declarations.
fc8a0ac3 5m7AMr18srnkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 93:82a7616e63686f727391da00402f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f636f672f6c73705f7365727665722e676f3a32333a312d35353a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10339 Guard that verifies an LSP server binary is available before use. Returns nil immediately when the requirement has no Locate probe. Takes a mutex around a package-level `locatedServers` map used as a memoization cache; only successful locations are cached, so a server installed mid-session is picked up on the next call without restarting. The locate probe runs under `context.WithoutCancel(ctx)` plus a fresh timeout so an already-cancelled caller context cannot make an installed server look missing. On success it records the name and returns nil. On failure it builds an error wrapping `lsp.ErrServerMissing` (so callers can errors.Is it): a 'not installed and offers no install command' message when InstallCommand is blank after trimming, otherwise a message telling the user which install command to run and to retry.
fc8a0ac3 8IAoPkdJAb_kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 134:82a7616e63686f727391da00692f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f676f2f706b672f6d6f642f676f6c616e672e6f72672f746f6f6c636861696e4076302e302e312d676f312e32362e332e64617277696e2d61726d36342f7372632f666d742f6572726f72732e676f3a32333a312d33323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d101d9 Formatted-error constructor split into two functions deliberately so both it and the `errors.New` fallback remain inlinable. It delegates to the unexported `errorf`, which returns a non-nil error only when the format string actually needed formatting/wrapping; that result is returned directly. If nil comes back, no formatting was required, so the format string is returned verbatim as `errors.New(format)`, skipping the allocations and work of building a formatted error.
fc8a0ac3 t3TqHNAmbC_kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 201: Dependency edge: the LSP server-requirement guard constructs its failure values through the formatted-error helper. Both error paths (no install command available, and install-command suggestion) call it with a `%w` verb wrapping the sentinel missing-server error, so the helper's formatting branch produces a wrapping error that preserves `errors.Is` matching against that sentinel for upstream callers.
fc8a0ac3 UNohYLw0u9DkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 137:82a7616e63686f727391da006c2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f676f2f706b672f6d6f642f676f6c616e672e6f72672f746f6f6c636861696e4076302e302e312d676f312e32362e332e64617277696e2d61726d36342f7372632f6572726f72732f6572726f72732e676f3a36343a312d36363a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d101b1 Constructor returning the `error` interface backed by a pointer to an unexported struct holding a single string field (Go stdlib `errors.New`). Returning a pointer means each call yields a distinct value: two errors created with identical text are never `==` equal, which makes such errors usable as unique sentinel values. The `errorString` type supplies `Error() string` returning the stored text, satisfying the `error` interface.
fc8a0ac3 ydfY3w8Rgo_kJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 204: A load/dependency edge: the first node pulls in (imports/references) the second node's definition, so the target must be resolved before the source can be evaluated. Here it reflects the error constructor depending on the struct type declaration it allocates and returns as an `error`.
fc8a0ac3 7jCtgioJaxnkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 106:82a7616e63686f727391da004d2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f6d63702f7365617263685f616e645f71756572795f746f6f6c2e676f3a3136313a362d3138343a34ac616e737765724c656e677468d1036a Worker closure run per matched file inside a bounded-concurrency fan-out: `defer wg.Done()` signals completion to the WaitGroup and `defer func(){ <-sem }()` releases a slot in the semaphore channel. It calls g.SearchSnippet(ctx, rel, snippet) to resolve the snippet into a graph plus its start/root node. On error it drops the per-file failure silently, except when errors.Is(err, lsp.ErrServerMissing) — that error is global (no language server means every file fails), so it is stashed into the shared `missing` variable under mutex so the caller can surface a meaningful reason instead of an empty result set. On success it appends, under the same mutex, a cog.GraphWithRoot{Graph, Root} to `pairs` and the relative path to `files`, keeping the two slices index-aligned. The mutex is required because multiple goroutines mutate the shared slices/variable concurrently.
fc8a0ac3 -YjYzTLt8QDkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 135:82a7616e63686f727391da006a2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f676f2f706b672f6d6f642f676f6c616e672e6f72672f746f6f6c636861696e4076302e302e312d676f312e32362e332e64617277696e2d61726d36342f7372632f6572726f72732f777261702e676f3a34353a312d35323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d101d3 Standard library errors.Is entry point: if either err or target is nil it degenerates to a plain equality check (`err == target`), true only when both are nil. Otherwise it uses reflectlite to test whether target's dynamic type is comparable — guarding against panics from `==` on uncomparable types like slices, maps, or funcs — and delegates to the unexported recursive `is`, which walks the error chain via Unwrap and honors any custom `Is(error) bool` method.
fc8a0ac3 -QU-h1Ea7MbkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 215: A load/dependency edge: the first unit references the second at load time, so the target symbol must be resolved before the caller can run. Concretely this matches the concurrent search worker depending on the errors matching helper — the worker's error branch invokes that comparison routine to classify the returned error (missing language server vs. per-file failure), making the error-matching definition a prerequisite symbol pulled in by the caller.
fc8a0ac3 4lA9WAfmS4zkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 101:82a7616e63686f727391da00482f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f74657374732f6c73705f726571756972656d656e745f746573742e676f3a32303a312d35323a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10489 Go test exercising cog.RequireLSPServer across four states. (1) An empty lsp.ServerRequirement is a no-op returning nil. (2) It reads the Golang language descriptor and asserts the requirement metadata is "gopls" with install command "go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest". It then overwrites req.Name with t.Name() so the process-wide memo of already-located servers isn't shared with other tests — each test gets a unique lookup key. (3) Missing-binary path: GOBIN points at a fresh t.TempDir() and PATH is narrowed via toolchainOnlyPath(t) so gopls cannot be found; the error must mention the binary name and the install command, and must wrap the sentinel lsp.ErrServerMissing — checked with ErrorIs rather than string matching because the search fans out over candidates and skips unresolvable ones, so the sentinel is how the real miss is identified. (4) Installed path: an empty 0o755 file named gopls is written into GOBIN, then RequireLSPServer is called with an already-cancelled context and must still return nil, ensuring cancellation is not misreported as a missing server (which would tell the user to install something already present).
fc8a0ac3 KmKAQ6OIQsbkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 131:82a7616e63686f727391da00662f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f76656e646f722f6769746875622e636f6d2f73747265746368722f746573746966792f6173736572742f617373657274696f6e732e676f3a323137323a312d323139343a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d1027a testify's assert.ErrorIs helper. It marks itself as a test helper when the TestingT implements Helper(), then returns true immediately if errors.Is(err, target) finds target anywhere in err's unwrap chain. On failure it computes expectedText from target.Error() (guarded against a nil target) and short-circuits with a dedicated "Expected error with %q in chain but got nil" message when err is nil while a target was expected. Otherwise it builds a printable unwrap chain via buildErrorChainString and reports a formatted expected-vs-chain failure through Fail. Returns a bool so callers can branch; it does not halt the test itself.
fc8a0ac3 5mqhPt46DIrkJbM_RyVeewAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 206: Dependency edge: the test function loads/calls the testify assertion helper. The sentinel check in the test resolves to that helper, which performs errors.Is unwrap-chain matching and, on mismatch, renders the expected target alongside the full error chain. This is what makes the assertion robust to wrapping — the resolver can wrap the missing-server sentinel with the binary name and install-command context, and the chain walk still matches.
fc8a0ac3 jHZMkXO_EnFyvi4gA2dxzAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f696e697469616c697a652f696e69742e676f3a31363a312d33363a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d1041d Builds and returns a configured *exec.Cmd without executing it, so no process is actually spawned here. Mutations: allocates a local args slice (a --append-system-prompt flag whose text embeds providers.QueryRoutingSystemPrompt(queries.Supported())) then appends the caller-supplied claudeArgs — claudeArgs is only read, append writes into the newly allocated slice, so the caller's slice is not mutated. It mutates the freshly created Cmd struct by assigning cmd.Env = providers.ClaudeEnv(). I/O: exec.Command performs a PATH lookup for the "claude" binary, which reads the PATH environment variable and stats filesystem entries (any error is stored on the Cmd); providers.ClaudeEnv() and queries.Supported() are external calls that likely read the process environment and a package-level registry of supported queries. No network, no file writes, no subprocess execution in this function. Concurrency: no goroutines, channels, or locks. Otherwise effectively side-effect free — the caller must Run/Start the returned command to cause real effects.
fc8a0ac3 jHZMkXO_EnHAW6nbcc8nEQAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 94:82a7616e63686f727391da00412f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f696e697469616c697a652f696e69742e676f3a31363a312d33363a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10363 Contract: takes a slice of extra CLI args (nil/empty is fine — then only the injected flag pair is passed) and always returns a non-nil *exec.Cmd for the `claude` binary; never returns an error and never executes the command. It unconditionally prepends `--append-system-prompt` plus a formatted instruction string whose %s is filled by the query-routing prompt built from the supported query list, then appends caller args after it, so caller args can never precede or displace the injected prompt. cmd.Env is replaced wholesale by the provider-supplied environment, meaning the child does NOT inherit os.Environ() unless that helper includes it. A missing `claude` executable is not surfaced here — exec.Command stores the lookup error in cmd.Err, which only appears at Start/Run time. An empty supported-query list still yields a valid (if less useful) prompt.
fc8a0ac3 -Wo4ym-voPDAW6nbcc8nEQAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 100:82a7616e63686f727391da00472f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f70726f7669646572732f636c617564655f636f64652e676f3a32323a312d33343a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10290 Contract: pure formatting function; accepts a slice of PromptQuery and always returns a non-empty string, never errors or panics on nil/empty input (nil slice simply yields the header line plus the trailing instruction sentence with no bullet entries). For each element it emits a `- <id>: <description>` line via the interface accessors; no validation, deduplication, or sorting is applied — output order mirrors input order and duplicate or empty IDs/descriptions pass through verbatim. A nil interface element inside the slice would panic on the method call, so callers must supply non-nil entries. No side effects; strings.Builder writes cannot fail.
